WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.386 --> 00:00:03.719
(cooking sounds medley)

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.529
- Breakfast time and I'm eating chips.

00:00:09.529 --> 00:00:12.362
(chill out music)

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:23.660
Okay so I made this video about

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.710
why Americans eat dessert for breakfast.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.470
- [Narrator] Two pancakes
with hot syrup and butter.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.410
- And in that video, I
said I would share with you

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.470
the non-American breakfast
I've started eating

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.220
if enough people commented,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.425
and let's just say,
enough people commented.

00:00:38.425 --> 00:00:39.803
(slamming sounds)

00:00:39.803 --> 00:00:40.636
(chime dinging)

00:00:40.636 --> 00:00:41.469
For the past several months,

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:43.360
I've been on this journey of inviting

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.240
different breakfast
traditions into my home

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.660
and into my habits,

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.790
and that's a hard thing to do,

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.860
but I've officially done it,

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.420
and I want to share it with you.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.760
So I'm gonna share with you my top three

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.430
non-American breakfast traditions.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.850
I wanna see in the comments

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.753
if you have suggestions on other breakfast

00:00:58.753 --> 00:01:01.910
that I could try out that I'm
not mentioning here, okay?

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.360
So leave those in the comments,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.052
for now, let's get into my
breakfast enlightenment.

00:01:07.052 --> 00:01:07.885
(chill out music)

00:01:07.885 --> 00:01:10.021
First up is Japanese.

00:01:10.021 --> 00:01:12.700
(chill out music)

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.050
I went to Japan for
the first time in 2017,

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.530
I was shooting an episode of Vox Borders,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.250
and I had just taken a 14-hour flight,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.760
I was totally delirious,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:22.910
I got off the flight,

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.530
I met up with my fixer in Tokyo

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.210
and it was morning time

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.630
and she was like, "Do
you wanna get breakfast?"

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.870
and I was like, "Yeah, sure."

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.141
So we showed up to a
place called Yoshinoya.

00:01:33.141 --> 00:01:33.974
- [Yoshi] Yoshi!

00:01:33.974 --> 00:01:34.807
- I walk into this place,

00:01:34.807 --> 00:01:35.680
I have no idea what to order,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.457
and my fixer is like, "Don't worry,

00:01:37.457 --> 00:01:39.120
"I'll order you something."

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.720
And she did, and before I knew it,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.561
out came a platter that looked like this.

00:01:44.561 --> 00:01:46.400
(chill out music)

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.980
It came with rice, and miso soup,

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.530
and a lightly grilled salted salmon

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.880
and a raw egg.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.550
Yes, a raw egg.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.550
I kind of freaked out
when I saw the raw egg,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.800
I was like, why didn't they cook the egg,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.530
isn't there salmonella in this egg?

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.910
Turns out in Japan,
they're just a lot better

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:04.360
about cleanliness and quality
control on their eggs,

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.210
not surprisingly, and because of that,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.170
they don't worry about salmonella

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.360
like we do in the United States.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.680
So it turns out, you can eat a raw egg.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.220
And I did and this meal turned out to be

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.730
one of the best meals of my life.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.810
I ate it every day during that trip.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.870
And here I am, three years later,

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.800
trying to reform my breakfast,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.289
and where do I turn but this meal.

00:02:25.289 --> 00:02:28.122
(chill out music)

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.640
Turns out, this meal is
called tamago kake gohan.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.210
Which basically means egg on rice,

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.970
and it is a very simple,
traditional Japanese breakfast.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.020
To recreate this,

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.240
I went to a wonderful
Japanese-Korean market

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.310
near my home,

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.300
and I went shopping for all of the goods.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.990
First thing I did was buy a rice cooker,

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.310
I didn't have a rice cooker before,

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.160
and I found out that a good rice cooker

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.710
makes all of the difference.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.710
Second up, good salmon.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.530
I kind of just throw some soy sauce

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.620
on the salmon, throw it in
the oven for 12 to 14 minutes,

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.690
and then wrap it up and
put it in my refrigerator,

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.060
and I eat it all week.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.430
So now my mornings with
Japanese breakfast mean

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.980
washing some rice, throwing
it in the rice cooker,

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.100
pulling it out 30 minutes later,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.920
and putting on a piece of
salmon, some sesame seed,

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.190
sometimes an avocado,
oftentimes a lot of ginger,

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.100
and all topped off with this amazing

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.080
Hello Kitty branded soy sauce,

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.700
that's like, kind of a morning
tamago kake gohan sauce

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:36.910
that I found at this market.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:37.970
It is delicious.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.030
It is unbelievably delicious
to have rice in the morning,

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.100
it's like salty, the salmon is salty,

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.910
the ginger is amazing,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.690
I put seaweed in it sometimes,

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.630
and I, like, when I'm eating that,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.550
I am just a happy happy human.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.870
I also make a big pot
of miso soup on Sundays,

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:58.350
which is a very easy soup,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:01.170
you can throw, like, six
ingredients into a pot,

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:02.390
let it sort of simmer for a bit,

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:04.210
and then you have miso soup
for the rest of the week.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.600
Oh and for the raw egg,
I end up just taking

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.020
a high-quality American raw egg

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.920
and whipping it into the hot rice,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.100
and it's delicious and I've
been eating it for months now

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.870
and I have not gotten sick,

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.630
so you can do it too.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.650
This food is so good.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:23.565
Next up, let's talk
about Israel-Palestine.

00:04:23.565 --> 00:04:26.690
(chill out music)

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:27.523
Okay, listen.

00:04:27.523 --> 00:04:30.050
Too much of my life has already
been spent in the comment

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.380
section of videos,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.540
reading and responding
to comments on videos

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.340
that I make about Israel-Palestine.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.130
Today, we're not talking
about the decades-old conflict

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.820
where one asymmetrical
power is illegally occupying

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.870
the other, ugh, jeeze,
see, you can't help it,

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.230
you just, you can't help it.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:50.900
Anyway, today we're
talking about breakfast.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.240
I was in Jerusalem in 2016 reporting

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:54.530
on the conflict,

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.310
and I was meeting up with
an Israeli journalist

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.230
who I had met online,
wanted to hang out with,

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.670
she told me to come to a breakfast spot,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.440
so I navigated to the breakfast spot,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.930
I parked, and I looked, and I realized,

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.420
it was a hummus restaurant.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:07.440
Hummus.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.520
For breakfast.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.570
I was little caught off-guard

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.940
because in my mind,
hummus looked like this.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.440
It was always like the extra add-on

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.030
that we would bring in a picnic

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.430
and in short, I didn't understand hummus.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.830
During that trip, I had hummus
of all kinds for breakfast,

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.370
in West Jerusalem, in East Jerusalem,

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.900
with falafel, with toppings of

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.620
different types of tahini, with mushrooms,

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:32.960
oh man, and every hummus was different,

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.050
and all of it was delicious.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:36.930
So fast forward three or four years

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:38.460
and I'm in my breakfast crisis,

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.730
I just genuinely do not want to wake up

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.930
and consume dessert for breakfast.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:49.103
And I decided that hummus is something

00:05:49.103 --> 00:05:49.936
that I want to invite into my home.

00:05:49.936 --> 00:05:51.070
A little daunting, 'cause I was like,

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.340
isn't hummus super complicated to make?

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.273
Turns out, it's not.

00:05:55.273 --> 00:05:57.190
(chill out music)

00:05:57.190 --> 00:06:00.360
Hummus is effectively
just chickpeas and tahini,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.940
which is ground up sesame seed.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.930
You can soak your own chickpeas,

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.570
which is incredibly cheap,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.380
or you can just buy
pre-soaked, canned chickpeas,

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:09.270
however you wanna do it.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.210
Basically what you're doing is combining

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.380
a bunch of ingredients into a blender,

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.210
and just blending it up,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.760
and you have hummus and you have hummus

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:17.630
for the entire week,

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:21.130
and it is a perfect
platform to add anything to,

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.930
I've been adding feta or an egg

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.900
or pairing it with pickles
and beautiful bread,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.680
I even tried my hand at making
pita bread for the first time

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.100
which is actually a pretty
simple, straightforward bread,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.570
I did it on a griddle
and in the oven, as well,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.870
both were fantastic, I mean,

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.780
this is just amazing food
to have in the morning.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.150
It's smooth and light but
also creamy, for some reason,

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.930
but it's not creamy like
it leaves you, like,

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.410
wanting to take a nap,
it's just good food.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.770
And now I make a big pot of hummus

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:51.610
at the beginning of the week,

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:52.790
and we have it for the whole week,

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.080
it stays good forever,

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.430
not actually forever, doesn't
actually stay good forever,

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.540
so, like, don't eat it after,
like, two, three months.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.452
Anyway, hummus is amazing.

00:07:02.452 --> 00:07:05.285
(chill out music)

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:14.400
Okay so the last breakfast
I wanna talk about today

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.000
comes from Texas, but really Mexico,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.150
I mean Texas was Mexico
and Mexico was Texas,

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.120
and then the US stole it all,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:24.440
and anyway I'm making a video about that,

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.157
but let's just talk about breakfast.

00:07:26.157 --> 00:07:27.480
(chill out music)

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.320
Last year, we were at South,
in South West, in Austin,

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:31.420
Iz was speaking,

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.670
and I was just tagging along,

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.400
and eating a million tacos.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.880
One morning, we walked
up to this taco truck.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.920
Vera Cruz all natural.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:42.760
Oof.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.480
Just saying the name makes my mouth water.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.840
Listen, I've eaten a
lot of tacos in my life.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.750
I lived in Northern Mexico for two years,

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.920
I ate a lot of tacos,
I continue to eat tacos

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:53.330
whenever I'm in that region,

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:57.550
and yet this morning those tacos were

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.670
some of the best tacos
I've ever had in my life.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.160
If not some of the best pieces of food

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.920
I've ever consumed.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.630
The tacos at Vera Cruz
are called migas tacos,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:10.000
which is whipping scrambled
eggs with corn chips,

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.590
like, crushed up corn chips,

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.250
and they kind of soften but
they retain their crunch

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:16.990
and they just make for
this delicious stuffing

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:19.160
into a taco, you can also add all pastor,

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:20.620
which is, like, pork,

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:23.340
and oh, just delicious.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.760
It's usually topped with cilantro,

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.790
onion, and of course, topped with

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:29.860
your choice of delicious salsa.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.530
All of this wrapped in a freshly made thin

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:35.380
but fatty flour tortilla.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.510
Flour tortilla, the key to
a delicious breakfast taco,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:39.640
is the flour tortilla.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.800
I'm a big fan of corn,
but a flour tortilla

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.330
in the morning is just amazing.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.340
This food wakes you up,

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.960
it fills you up, and
it just makes you happy

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:51.320
to be alive when you're eating it.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.940
I know it sounds super dramatic,

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.120
but I'm telling you, man,

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:58.660
I have a very strong
affinity to breakfast tacos.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:01.030
Inviting this in my home
was not a hard thing to do

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.820
because I've been making
Mexican-inspired food

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:05.985
for many years, ever
since I lived in Mexico,

00:09:05.985 --> 00:09:08.010
and what I love about
it is it's simple food.

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:11.420
It is eggs, and chips, and tortilla,

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:12.950
and you could throw cheese on there,

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:14.090
and an avocado.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:16.923
(chill out music)

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:23.260
Okay, so those are my three
new staples in my life,

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.130
Japanese, hummus, and breakfast tacos.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:28.350
I promised I'd tell you
which of these is my favorite

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:32.940
and I have to say that
Japanese takes the cake.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:33.773
Cake.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.570
There's something about
having rice in the morning,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.680
it's just, it fills you up,
it stays with you for hours

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.150
and I love breakfast.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.510
Never thought I'd say it but I
love freakin' love breakfast.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:46.460
So remember, if you haven't already,

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:47.930
go in the comments and tell me

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:49.650
if you have a non-American breakfast idea

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:51.820
that I can start trying out in my life,

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:54.340
give as much detail or a link as possible,

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.330
I would love to see what other
people eat for breakfast,

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.290
all of these ideas that I got came

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:02.110
from just casual interactions with people

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:03.170
who live in other countries

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:05.690
who do have different breakfast paradigms

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:07.520
and I hope that reading
through the comments

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:08.920
I can get some more ideas.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:10.400
So thanks for watching.

